<header><p class="st-kicker">princely state · Indian princely states</p><h1 class="st-title">Morvi State</h1><p class="st-meta">1698 CE – 1948 CE (approx.) · capital Morbi</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Jadeja Rajput state of Kathiawar founded by a displaced heir of Kutch, later known for its railways, port and tile and clock industries.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Morvi was founded in 1698 by Kanyoji, the eldest son of the Rao of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/cutch-state">Kutch</a>, who had been passed over for the Kutch throne. He established himself on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/machchhu-river">Machhu</a> river in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kathiawar">Kathiawar</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The Thakore Sahibs ruled a territory of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a> farms with a port on the Gulf of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/cutch-state">Kutch</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/waghji-ii">Waghji</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lakhdhirji-waghji">Lakhdhirji</a> in the late 19th and early 20th centuries built a light railway, tramway and Navlakhi port and fostered local industry. It was an eleven-gun salute state.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Morvi merged into the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/saurashtra-state">United State of Saurashtra</a> in 1948 and became part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gujarat">Gujarat</a> in 1960. Morbi is now a centre of ceramic tiles and clocks.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/morvi-state.jpg" alt="Morvi State" width="320" height="186"><figcaption>Image: Xufanc · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Morvi_State.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1698 CE – 1948 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>princely state</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Morbi</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Kanyoji, Waghji, Lakhdhirji</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Hinduism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Gujarati</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>India</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Morbi</dd></dl></aside></div>
